{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["And it says concerning some of the plagues, <b>Get up early in the morning, behold he goes out to the water</b>. And according to the plain sense, it was at the time when kings go out in the morning to stroll by the water, and HaShem commanded Moses to go there. And the reason for <b>“by blood”</b> is that since it was the first plague, He wanted him to perform it before his eyes and without fear of him. And this is the reason for <b>“and you shall station yourself to meet him”</b> (Exodus 7:15), <b>“and present yourself before Pharaoh”</b> (Exodus 8:16) — that he should stand opposite him with a raised hand. So too with the plague of <b>'arov</b> (mixed wild beasts) it says <b>“Get up early in the morning and present yourself before Pharaoh, behold he goes out to the water.”</b> And with the hail (Exodus 9:13): <b>“Get up early in the morning and present yourself before Pharaoh”</b> — and this too is when he goes out to the water. For with the <b>'arov</b> and the hail, in which there is death and punishment for human beings, HaShem wanted it to be before the eyes of all the people, for when the king goes out to the water, most of the people go after him, and He would warn him before their eyes — perhaps they will cry out to their master to turn back from his evil way, and if they do not do so, they will be liable to be punished. But with the rest of the plagues, a warning to the king alone suffices, therefore it says concerning them <b>“Come to Pharaoh”</b> (above 7:26, below 9:1, 10:1), that he should come to his palace. And with the lice and the boils He did not mention thus, because Aaron was needed to strike the dust, and in the palace of the king there is no dust, for it is a pavement of alabaster and marble; and so Moses was needed to throw the soot heavenward. And these two were performed before Pharaoh while he was in the courtyard of the king's garden-palace or something similar."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :notes {0 "The phrase 'והנה יתרה אותו לעיניהם' is rendered hyper-literally as 'He would warn him before their eyes'; the subject is HaShem and the object is Pharaoh, reading 'יתרה' from the root י-ת-ר in the sense of 'warn/admonish,' though the form is unusual."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:05:26.673003681Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}